# Section 1 — LLMs, RAG, Embeddings, Evaluation & Safety

### Questions 1–25

## 1. Walk me through an LLM application you've built or worked on.

### Strong interview answer

> One of the main LLM applications I've worked on is Lingo, which is a multi-client conversational AI platform. At a high level, users interact with a common interface, but the application needs to provide responses based on the specific client or domain they're authorized to access.
>
> When a user authenticates, we establish their identity and client context. When they submit a query, the request first goes through a primary orchestration layer. That layer determines which client-specific assistant or capability should handle the request.
>
> Once routed, the client-specific assistant can use Retrieval-Augmented Generation rather than relying purely on the model's pretrained knowledge. We retrieve relevant information from approved client knowledge sources, provide that information to the LLM as context, and instruct the model to answer based on that context.
>
> The reason that architecture is valuable is that it separates orchestration from domain-specific behavior. Instead of maintaining one enormous prompt containing every client's rules and knowledge, individual assistants can have their own configuration, knowledge sources, prompts and capabilities.
>
> From an engineering standpoint, I've been involved with the application layer around LLM orchestration, RAG, LangChain/LangGraph-style workflows, model integration and the production software concerns around those components.

### What they're testing

They want to know whether you actually understand:

**input → routing → retrieval → model → validation → response**

Don't spend five minutes describing the UI.

